+## 9. Deeper finding (2026-10-16, later): the two lines diverged in BEHAVIOUR, not just structure
+
+Reviewing the remaining 13 conflicts directly, several are not structural at all —
+they are genuine behavioural divergences in production code, so a merge cannot be
+resolved by "take one side's file structure":
+
+- `src/modules/chatqueue/outbox/flush.ts`: main flushes only `pending` entries
+  ("a 'sent' entry is done, and a 'failed' entry is only retried once the caller
+  requeues it"); the release line flushes everything except `sent`/`conflicted`
+  (resubmitting `sending`/`failed`). These are opposite retry semantics. Main's
+  tree even carries `ea9a7648 Fix outbox flush resubmitting failed messages`,
+  i.e. main *deliberately* chose the narrower behaviour the release line lacks.
+- `src/modules/views/admin/DefaultsView.tsx`: main moved `DefaultViews` into
+  `./defaults.ts` and added `SharePanel`; the release line instead grew an inline
+  `DefaultViews` interface (route/label/users/selected/onApply). Reconciliation
+  needs a product decision, not a union.
+- Test infrastructure (capstore, secret-url/outgoing, islands/append): the two
+  lines independently *refactored the same directories in opposite directions*
+  (nested `harness/` + `fixtures/` barrels on main vs flattened per-file modules
+  on release), producing duplicate fixture files in the merged tree.
+
+Conclusion: a wholesale release→main merge is **not** a mechanical integration.
+It would force unilateral choices between two lines' behavioural fixes (outbox
+retry semantics among them) on the production deploy branch. Recommendation
+stands and is now stronger: land the specific in-scope Kawun work (board-13
+`src/grants/policy/*`) as a fresh, focused change on main, or decide that main has
+superseded the release line. Do not merge wholesale.
+
+Paused state preserved: `/workspace/agent-195-integration/repo`, branch
+`integration/195-release-onto-main`, mid-merge vs `a14856a3`, 13 conflicts.
